# a voxel engine, part 4: making depth visible

The basic renderer does not store depth. It draws the terrain front to back and keeps track of the highest pixel per column. Everything behind that is skipped. For a heightmap, this is usually enough.

The problem appears once the renderer is no longer strictly uniform. To stay fast, it advances in depth spans. Parts of the map are skipped, others sampled more densely. As a result, there is no strict relation between a screen position and a single world position anymore.

Once something is drawn, its depth is implicit and cannot be recovered.

That makes it difficult to add anything on top of the terrain. There is no reliable way to decide whether something should be in front or behind.

The solution is a small depth buffer. Not at full resolution, but at the same reduced resolution as the renderer. One value per block.

While drawing the terrain, the current depth is already known and constant for each span. Writing it into the buffer costs almost nothing: a single byte store per block. Memory usage is small, and there is no additional computation in the inner loop.

Rendering then becomes a simple comparison against that buffer. Draw only if the new depth is closer, and update the stored value.

This removes the dependency on draw order. Visibility becomes local and consistent.

A similar idea appears in Ecstatica, where ellipsoids are rendered against a depth map. Different geometry, but the same principle: once depth is explicit, everything else becomes simpler.

In this engine, terrain, objects, and effects all use the same buffer.

